<%#
    The cookies this site stores, said plainly. TWO now: the session cookie, which
    is essential, and the theme cookie, which remembers an appearance choice. Both
    are named, because a banner that says "one cookie" while setting two is not
    telling the truth.

    Declining is not offered, because neither cookie is optional: the session one
    keeps you signed in, and the theme one is the only way a stored choice can be
    applied before the page paints. There is no analytics and no advertising.

    INCLUDED FROM `theme-head`, so it reaches every page rather than the one view it used to
    live in. That was the whole defect: the markup and the script were inline in
    `admin-login.ejs`, so on the console, the roadmap, the questionnaire and the rest there was
    no banner at all - which the owner met as "not clickable", because there was nothing there
    to click. `theme-head` is the same place the theme picker is defined, and for the same
    reason: it is site chrome, not a page's content.

    THE SCRIPT IS INLINE HERE RATHER THAN IN A `<script src>`, deliberately. It must run on
    every page that renders the banner, and a partial that carries both means a view cannot
    include one without the other. A separate file would be a second thing to remember at ten
    call sites, and the failure mode of forgetting it is silent - a banner that never appears,
    or appears and cannot be dismissed.
%>
<div id="cookie-banner" class="cookie-banner hidden">
    <div class="cookie-inner">
        <strong>This site stores two cookies.</strong>
        One keeps you signed in to the admin console, and one remembers your theme choice.
        Both are essential to how the site works, so they are set either way. There are no
        analytics and no advertising cookies.
        <div class="cookie-actions">
            <button id="cookie-ok">Understood</button>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

<script>
    // The banner records only that it was seen, in localStorage rather than a
    // cookie, so acknowledging it does not itself set one. Sign-in does not depend
    // on this running: the banner is hidden until the script shows it.
    (function () {
        var banner = document.getElementById('cookie-banner');
        if (!banner) return;

        // RESERVES THE SPACE THE BANNER OCCUPIES, so it does not sit ON the page's content.
        // It is `position: fixed; bottom: 0`, so it covers whatever is under it - and on the
        // questionnaire that is the answer input and its send button, measured at 390px: the
        // banner is 177px tall and `elementFromPoint` at the input's centre returns the
        // banner, so a participant cannot type. The height is read from the banner itself
        // rather than written as a constant, because it wraps to a different number of lines
        // at different widths and a fixed padding would be wrong at some viewport.
        function reserveSpace() {
            if (banner.classList.contains('hidden')) {
                document.documentElement.style.removeProperty('--cookie-notice-height');
                return;
            }
            document.documentElement.style.setProperty(
                '--cookie-notice-height', banner.offsetHeight + 'px'
            );
        }

        try {
            if (!localStorage.getItem('fr_cookie_notice')) banner.classList.remove('hidden');
        } catch (err) {
            // localStorage can be unavailable (private mode, disabled storage). The
            // safe degradation is to show the notice, not to hide it.
            banner.classList.remove('hidden');
        }
        reserveSpace();

        document.getElementById('cookie-ok').addEventListener('click', function () {
            try { localStorage.setItem('fr_cookie_notice', '1'); } catch (err) { /* storage unavailable */ }
            banner.classList.add('hidden');
            reserveSpace();
        });

        // THE HEIGHT CHANGES WITH THE VIEWPORT, so the reserved space is recomputed when the
        // banner re-wraps. Without this a phone turned sideways keeps the portrait height and
        // leaves a gap, or a desktop resized down keeps a height that is now too small.
        window.addEventListener('resize', reserveSpace);
    })();
</script>
